[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath target 64x64 R32_Float, operands 0.015625 to 1"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"a float travels through the uniform buffer unaltered\": 0 units in the last place: nothing is computed, the value is copied through a uniform buffer and written out again"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"addition is correctly rounded\": 0 units in the last place: the core table requires addition and multiplication to be correctly rounded, so there is one right answer, and the reference reaches it exactly because the sum and the product of two single precision numbers are exact in double precision"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"multiplication is correctly rounded\": 0 units in the last place: the core table requires addition and multiplication to be correctly rounded, so there is one right answer, and the reference reaches it exactly because the sum and the product of two single precision numbers are exact in double precision"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"a multiply and an add in one call\": 0 units in the last place: the console's binary is one fused FFMA, which rounds once, and the reference is the processor's fused multiply and add, the same single rounding. The table would let an unfused pair round twice, up to 1.5 units off; a machine that splits it differs from the console, and the case says so"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"division within two and a half units in the last place\": 3 units in the last place: 2.5 for division in the core table, plus the half the reference carries for being a double precision answer rounded to single"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the square root\": 5 units in the last place: the table gives the square root no figure of its own and inherits it from one over the inverse square root, which is 2 for the inverse square root and 2.5 for the division, plus the half the reference carries. Three would be a number from somewhere else"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"two to the power of\": 3.5 plus 2 per unit of the argument: the table allows three plus twice the magnitude of the argument, and the half the reference carries goes on the constant. Over the range this case sweeps that is three and a half at the smallest argument and eleven and a half at the largest, and a flat three would fail a machine that was inside its allowance everywhere but the middle"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the logarithm to base two\": 3.5 units in the last place: 3 from the table, which is the figure that applies outside the range from a half to two, where this operand is kept, plus the half the reference carries"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the sine, within two to the minus eleven\": 0.000488281 absolute, which is two to the minus eleven: the table bounds these two by an absolute error inside minus pi to pi and not by units in the last place at all. The shading language's own specification leaves them undefined, which is the clearest reason this suite holds itself to the other one"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the cosine, within two to the minus eleven\": 0.000488281 absolute, which is two to the minus eleven: the table bounds these two by an absolute error inside minus pi to pi and not by units in the last place at all. The shading language's own specification leaves them undefined, which is the clearest reason this suite holds itself to the other one"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"fma keeps the product's rounding error, as the console's FFMA does\": 0 units in the last place: the console's FFMA is fused, and the exact product less a nearby float is exact itself, so there is one answer. Done apart the product rounds first and the answer loses its low bits, which is a difference from the console"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"min and max against infinities\": 0 units in the last place: a minimum or a maximum only picks one of its operands, and against an infinity which one is defined"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"min and max hand back the operand that is not a NaN\": 0 units in the last place: the console's minimum and maximum hand back the operand that is not a NaN, as GLSL.std.450's NMin and NMax define it. The plain FMin a translator may reach for leaves this undefined, so a NaN here is a difference from the console, which is the machine an emulator answers to"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"mix carries infinities and NaNs through\": 0 units in the last place: each answer is the infinity or the NaN that x times one less a plus y times a gives in IEEE arithmetic, and x plus a times y less x gives the same one. Vulkan lets a machine assume no infinity or NaN arrives, so a failure is a difference from the console rather than from the table"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"denormal products, sums and operands flush, as the console's do\": 0 units in the last place: every instruction in the console's binary flushes denormals, operands and results, so each answer is exact and one. The table allows keeping them; a machine that does differs from the console, and the case says so"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"two to the power of -128 to nearly 128\": 3.5 plus 2 per unit of the argument: the power of two's own figure, over -128 to nearly 128, which reaches 259.5 at the top. Below two to the -126 the console's MUFU flushes the answer, so nought is the answer there"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the logarithm from the smallest normal to nearly the largest float\": 3.5 units in the last place: the logarithm's 3 and the half the reference carries, on operands from the smallest normal to nearly the largest float with a full mantissa, none inside a half to two where the table switches to an absolute figure"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the square root of denormals and tiny normals\": 5 units in the last place: the square root's figure, on operands from the denormals to two to the -96. The console's MUFU flushes a denormal operand, so nought is the answer for those"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"the inverse square root of denormals and tiny normals\": 2.5 units in the last place: 2 from the table and the half the reference carries. The console's MUFU flushes a denormal operand, so infinity is the answer for those"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"floor of negatives and fractions\": 0 units in the last place: the answer is a whole number or the exact difference between the operand and one, so there is one right answer and the table's are exact"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"fract of negatives\": 0 units in the last place: the answer is a whole number or the exact difference between the operand and one, so there is one right answer and the table's are exact"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"round of negatives and halves, halves to even as the console's\": 0 units in the last place, and a half to even: the language lets round go either way there, and the console's binary compiles it to the same rounding as roundEven, so a machine that rounds a half away from nought differs from the console. So does one that rounds -0.25 to plus nought, where the console's F2F keeps the sign"}
[game] {"type":"note","text":"fmath budget for \"roundEven of halves goes to the even neighbour\": 0 units in the last place: the answer is a whole number, so the table's is exact. The sign of a nought is the console's rule: its F2F rounds -0.25 and -0.5 to minus nought, and a machine that gives plus nought differs from the console"}
